Files distributed with MSstatsConvertLLM
========================================

AlphaPept_subset.csv
AlphaPept_annotation.csv
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

AlphaPept output for the example in the vignette "2. Converting the output
of a tool without a converter". MSstatsConvert has no AlphaPept converter, so
the vignette converts this file with a mapping proposed by a language model.

Source
  ProteoBench, file test/data/quant/quant_lfq_ion_DDA_QExactive/
  AlphaPept_subset.csv.
  https://github.com/Proteobench/ProteoBench
  Retrieved 27 October 2026.

License
  The ProteoBench repository, including this file, is distributed under the
  Apache License 2.0. https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

Underlying measurements
  A Q Exactive HF-X DDA subset of the mixed-species LFQ benchmark of

    Van Puyvelde, B. et al. A comprehensive LFQ benchmark dataset on modern
    day acquisition strategies in proteomics. Scientific Data 9, 126 (2022).
    https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01216-6

  deposited in PRIDE as PXD028735. The article is open access under CC BY 4.0.

Experimental design
  Human, yeast and E. coli digests are mixed in two proportions, conditions A
  and B. ProteoBench uses the first biological replicate series, "alpha",
  with three technical replicates of each condition, six runs in all. Human is
  present in equal amounts in both conditions, yeast is twice as abundant in
  A, and E. coli is four times as abundant in B, so the expected log2 fold
  changes of B against A are 0, -1 and +2.

  AlphaPept_annotation.csv lists the six runs. Each run has its own
  BioReplicate, so MSstats compares A with B across the three runs of each
  condition, as ProteoBench does. The BioReplicate labels include the
  condition, since a BioReplicate that appears in both conditions would make
  MSstats treat the design as paired.

Subset
  To keep the package small, the subset contains every row of a protein seen
  in at least four of the six runs, and every row flagged as a decoy, which a
  filter-aware prompt should find. This leaves 7,106 of the 9,814 rows, 462
  proteins and all 44 decoy rows, in 851 kB. The columns are unchanged.

  Rebuild with: Rscript inst/scripts/make_new_format_data.R

new_format_mapping.rds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The mapping that deepseek-r1-14b returned for AlphaPept_subset.csv under the
filter_aware prompt. It was obtained once, with Ollama on a local computer,
and is distributed so that the vignette runs without a language model.

  Rebuild with: Rscript inst/scripts/make_new_format_mapping.R

raw_trials_benchmark_20261007_163702.rds
downstream_panels.rds
benchmark_columns.rds
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The saved results read by the vignette "1. Reproducing the benchmark of models
and prompts": the 288 benchmark trials, the per-protein adjusted p-values of
the differential abundance comparisons in the manuscript, and the column
names of each public dataset. They were produced from the runs in results/ by
inst/scripts/make_extdata.R.
